    <h1 class="rmd-hero-h1">
      RMD Calculator: Calculate Your Required Minimum Distribution
    </h1>
    <p class="rmd-hero-date">Figures last verified against official sources in July 2026.</p>
    <div class="rmd-aeo-block" style="background: #0A3D3A; border-radius: 0.75rem; padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem; margin: 1.25rem 0;">
      <p style="font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: 'uppercase'; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: #C9933A; margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;">Quick Answer</p>
      <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #ffffff; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">Required Minimum Distributions begin at age 73 under the SECURE 2.0 Act (2026). Your RMD equals your prior year-end IRA balance divided by the IRS Uniform Lifetime Table life expectancy factor for your age. A $500,000 IRA at age 73 has a factor of 26.5, giving an RMD of $18,868.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="rmd-who-for" style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 1rem 0 0 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem;">
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-text-muted, #4B5A6E);"><span aria-hidden="true" style="color: #0A3D3A; font-weight: 700; flex-shrink: 0;">→</span> Retirees aged 73 or older who must take their first or annual RMD from a traditional IRA, 401(k), or 403(b) and want to avoid the 25% excise tax for missing it.</li>
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-text-muted, #4B5A6E);"><span aria-hidden="true" style="color: #0A3D3A; font-weight: 700; flex-shrink: 0;">→</span> Adults turning 73 this year who want to calculate their RMD before December 31 and understand the first-year April 1 extension option.</li>
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-text-muted, #4B5A6E);"><span aria-hidden="true" style="color: #0A3D3A; font-weight: 700; flex-shrink: 0;">→</span> Adult children or financial caregivers helping an elderly parent determine the correct RMD amount from inherited or owned retirement accounts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="rmd-hero-sub">
      Calculate your exact Required Minimum Distribution (RMD) dollar amount for {currentYear}. Free — no signup, no account, no email required. Automatically updated using current IRS Uniform Lifetime Table III.
    </p>
    <p class="rmd-hero-source">
      Verified against IRS Publication 590-B &amp; SECURE 2.0 Act rules. Last reviewed: {lastReviewed}. Not financial or tax advice.
    </p>
  </div>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       2. INTERACTIVE TOOL (FRONT AND CENTER)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="rmd-tool-block" id="calculator">
    <RMDCalculatorEvergreen client:load />
  </div>

  <NoSignupStatement />

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       3. PLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="rmd-section" aria-labelledby="explanation-heading">
    <h2 id="explanation-heading">What Your RMD Means — and Why It Matters</h2>

    <p>
      A <strong>Required Minimum Distribution (RMD)</strong> is the mandatory minimum dollar amount that the IRS requires you to withdraw each year from tax-deferred retirement accounts, such as Traditional IRAs, 401(k)s, 403(b)s, and SEP IRAs.
    </p>

    <h3>SECURE 2.0 Age Rules (Age 73 vs. Age 75)</h3>
    <p>
      The SECURE 2.0 Act enacted by Congress updated the required starting age for RMDs based on your birth year:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Born between 1951 and 1959:</strong> Your RMD starting age is <strong>73</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Born in 1960 or later:</strong> Your RMD starting age is <strong>75</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to Calculate RMD (The Official IRS Formula)</h3>
    <p>
      Your annual RMD is calculated using a simple formula mandated by IRS Publication 590-B:
    </p>
    <div class="rmd-formula-box">
      <strong>RMD = Prior Year Dec 31 Account Balance ÷ IRS Distribution Factor</strong>
    </div>

    <p>
      The IRS distribution factor is based on your age in the current calendar year. As you grow older, your distribution factor decreases, which increases the percentage of your portfolio you must withdraw.
    </p>

    <div class="rmd-info-box">
      <p class="rmd-info-box-heading">Beware of the "Double-RMD" Tax Trap in Year One</p>
      <p>
        For your very first RMD year, the IRS allows you to delay your withdrawal until <strong>April 1 of the following year</strong>. However, if you wait until April 1, you must take your first RMD AND your second RMD in the same tax year. This double withdrawal can spike your taxable income and trigger higher Medicare IRMAA surcharges.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       4. WORKED EXAMPLE (STEP BY STEP)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="rmd-section rmd-section--tinted" aria-labelledby="worked-example-heading">
    <h2 id="worked-example-heading">Step-by-Step Worked Example: Eleanor's RMD</h2>
    <p>
      Here is how the <strong>required minimum distribution calculator</strong> computes an annual RMD for a 74-year-old retiree.
    </p>

    <div class="rmd-example-card">
      <div class="rmd-example-scenario">
        <span class="rmd-example-label">Scenario</span>
        <p>
          <strong>Eleanor</strong> turned 74 years old in {currentYear}. As of December 31 of last year, her Traditional IRA balance was <strong>$400,000</strong>. Her husband is 72 years old (not more than 10 years younger).
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="rmd-example-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Find Prior Year Balance:</strong>
          $400,000.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lookup IRS Life Expectancy Factor (Table III):</strong>
          For age 74, the official IRS Uniform Lifetime Table distribution factor is <strong>25.5</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Calculate Annual RMD:</strong>
          $400,000 ÷ 25.5 = <strong>$15,686.27</strong> (rounded to $15,686).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Calculate Monthly Equivalent:</strong>
          $15,686 ÷ 12 = <strong>$1,307 per month</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deadline:</strong>
          Eleanor must complete her withdrawal of $15,686 by <strong>December 31, {currentYear}</strong> to avoid the 25% IRS penalty ($3,921.50 penalty).
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div style="background: #fff8f0; border-left: 4px solid #E8761A; border-radius: 0 0.5rem 0.5rem 0; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 700; color: #E8761A; margin: 0 0 0.25rem 0;">💡 Pro Tip</p>
        <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">If you have multiple IRAs, you calculate your RMD separately for each account — but you can withdraw the combined RMD total from any one or combination of your IRAs. This flexibility lets you consolidate distributions strategically. For 401(k)s, each account must take its own RMD separately.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       5. COMMON PITFALLS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="rmd-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Delaying Your First RMD to April 1 Without Accounting for the Double-RMD</h3>
    <p>The IRS allows you to delay your very first RMD to April 1 of the year after you turn 73. However, if you do this, you must also take your second RMD by December 31 of that same year — resulting in two taxable distributions in one year, which can spike your IRMAA bracket.</p>
    <p>The fix is to take your first RMD in the year you turn 73 unless your accountant has specifically modeled the two-RMD scenario.</p>

    <h3>Forgetting That Multiple IRA Accounts Each Have Separate RMDs</h3>
    <p>If you have three Traditional IRAs, you technically have three RMDs. However, the IRS allows you to aggregate them and take the total from one account. 401(k)s cannot be aggregated with IRAs — each must have its RMD taken separately.</p>
    <p>The fix is to list every tax-deferred account, calculate each RMD amount, then aggregate only the IRA amounts.</p>

    <h3>Missing the Calendar-Year Deadline</h3>
    <p>All RMDs after your first must be taken by December 31 of the calendar year. Many retirees wait until January and are assessed the 25% penalty.</p>
    <p>The fix is to set a standing calendar reminder for November 15 each year to verify your RMD has been completed or is in progress.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
